# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Terragram — environment.  Copy to `.env.local`, then `npm run dev`.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Section 1 = the app won't boot without it. 2 = billing. Everything under OPTIONAL
# is OFF until you uncomment + fill it. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` are baked into the browser
# at BUILD time — restart `next dev` after changing one.


# ─── 1. REQUIRED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supabase → your project → Project Settings:
#   URL              = Settings → Data API → "Project URL"
#   publishable key  = Settings → API Keys → "Publishable"  (sb_publishable_…)
#   secret key       = Settings → API Keys → "Secret"       (sb_secret_…, server-only)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-REF.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_REPLACE_ME
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_REPLACE_ME

# Your public origin. Local = leave as-is. Prod = your Railway domain (https://…).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000


# ─── 2. BILLING (Stripe) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EXACTLY two keys + one command — that is the entire setup. Prices and the meter
# are created by the script and resolved automatically (no ids to paste, and there
# is NO Stripe publishable key — the app has no client-side Stripe).
#
#   STRIPE_SECRET_KEY     = Stripe → Developers → API keys → "Secret key"  (use sk_test_… first)
#   STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET = Stripe → Developers → Webhooks → add endpoint
#                           <your-url>/api/webhooks → copy its "Signing secret" (whsec_…).
#                           Locally instead run `stripe login --project-name=terragram` & `npm run stripe:listen` — it prints one.
#   then, once:             npm run stripe:setup      (creates the catalogue; re-run safe)
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_REPLACE_ME
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_REPLACE_ME
# OPTIONAL. `true` requests UK/EU VAT (automatic_tax + VAT-id collection) on
# subscription checkout/upgrade. Activate Stripe Tax in the dashboard FIRST (per mode)
# or checkout errors. Leave unset until then. Credit top-ups stay untaxed by design.
# STRIPE_TAX_ENABLED=true


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OPTIONAL — one block = one feature. Leave commented to keep it OFF.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Google Analytics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off until set; then loads only AFTER the visitor clicks Accept on the cookie banner.
# Google Analytics → Admin → Data Streams → your stream → "Measurement ID".
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX

# ── Invite email (Amazon SES) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL. Until BOTH keys are set, no email is attempted: an invite is a link the
# Admin copies from /members, and the dialog says the email was not sent. Set them
# and the invitee is also emailed the link (a failed send never fails the invite).
#   1) SES (in SES_REGION) → Verified identities → verify the domain of the sender
#      (terragram.dev: DKIM CNAMEs + a custom MAIL FROM is best) and request
#      production access, or SES only delivers to verified addresses (sandbox).
#   2) IAM → a send-only user, policy `ses:SendEmail` on that identity →
#      create an access key → the two values below.
# SES_ACCESS_KEY_ID=AKIA_REPLACE_ME
# SES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=REPLACE_ME
# SES_REGION=eu-west-2                             # defaults to eu-west-2 (London)
# EMAIL_FROM="Terragram <invites@terragram.dev>"  # a VERIFIED SES identity; defaults to this

# ── GitHub sign-in (OAuth) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL. Adds a "Continue with GitHub" button on login + signup. It stays HIDDEN
# until this flag is `true`, so you never ship a button before the provider exists.
# This is SIGN-IN ONLY and is unrelated to the "Open pull request" Git connection
# at the bottom of this file — those are separate credentials.
#   1) GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → OAuth Apps → New OAuth App.
#      Authorisation callback URL: https://<YOUR-REF>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
#   2) Supabase → Authentication → Providers → GitHub → enable, paste both, save.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GITHUB_OAUTH_ENABLED=true

# ── Google sign-in (OAuth) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL. Adds a "Continue with Google" button on login + signup. It stays HIDDEN
# until this flag is `true`, so you never ship a button before the provider exists.
# Two steps, both required:
#   1) Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → Create OAuth client ID
#      (type "Web application"). Authorised redirect URI:
#         https://<YOUR-REF>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
#      Copy the Client ID + Client secret.
#   2) Supabase → Authentication → Providers → Google → enable, paste both, save.
# Then set this and restart `next dev` (NEXT_PUBLIC_* is baked in at build time):
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_OAUTH_ENABLED=true

# ── Managed-model proxy (Bedrock inference billing, EU-resident) ──────────────────
# OPTIONAL. Until set, managed inference is off and the workspace runs on the
# deterministic engine. It is a SEPARATE service — the `terragram-proxy/` sibling —
# deployed to AWS so its Bedrock creds never touch this app. To stand it up:
#   1) openssl rand -hex 32                         # generate the shared secret; KEEP it
#   2) cd ../terragram-proxy/terraform
#      terraform init -backend-config=...           # S3 backend (bucket/key/region)
#      terraform apply -var="proxy_secret=<the hex from step 1>"
#   3) copy the printed `proxy_function_url`, then set both here (and on Railway):
# TERRAGRAM_MODEL_PROXY_URL=https://<proxy_function_url>
# TERRAGRAM_MODEL_PROXY_SECRET=<the same hex from step 1>     # SIGNS the tmpx_ token
#
# OPTIONAL hardening: a SEPARATE secret that authenticates the proxy's usage
# callback, so leaking one secret no longer grants both "forge a tmpx_ token" AND
# "pass the callback". If unset, the callback reuses TERRAGRAM_MODEL_PROXY_SECRET
# (works, but warn-logs). To SPLIT them, generate a fresh value and set it BOTH here
# and on the proxy (terraform apply -var="proxy_callback_secret=<this>"):
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# TERRAGRAM_PROXY_CALLBACK_SECRET=<a different hex>

# ── Managed workspace inference (Chat → Model via the proxy above) ────────────────
# OPTIONAL. Requires the proxy block. `managed` routes /api/workspace/translate
# through UK Bedrock (managed-entitled orgs only); any gateway error falls back to
# the deterministic engine, so availability never depends on it.
# WORKSPACE_ENGINE=managed
# TERRAGRAM_WORKSPACE_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5    # the Bedrock model the proxy maps

# ── Rate-limit trusted proxy hops ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL. How many trusted proxies sit in front of the app — the client IP for
# per-IP rate-limit keys is read that many hops in from the RIGHT of x-forwarded-for
# (the left is client-spoofable). Default 1 = Railway's single edge; raise only if you
# add another proxy/CDN in front. The rate limiter's counters live in Postgres
# (`consume_rate_limit`) whenever SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY is set, so they hold across
# replicas and restarts; without it they are in-process.
# RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_PROXIES=1

# ── Scheduled jobs (the 15-minute managed-usage flush to Stripe) ──────────────────
# OPTIONAL. Until set, POST /api/jobs/flush-usage refuses every caller (401) and
# managed usage reaches Stripe only at renewal or on a billing-page view. You invent
# the secret; `npm run cron:schedule` puts the SAME value in the Supabase Vault for
# the `terragram-flush-usage` pg_cron job (see DEPLOY.md).
# JOBS_DRAIN_SECRET=     # `openssl rand -hex 24` (≥16 chars, or the route refuses it)

# ── Evidence signing (Ed25519 signature on each evidence receipt) ────────────────
# The review gate's "Generate" records an approval and a SHA-256 content hash over
# the model + plan; with this key set the receipt also carries a detached Ed25519
# signature (unset ⇒ the receipt honestly reports "unsigned").
# Generate: openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out evidence.pem  → paste the PEM.
# EVIDENCE_SIGNING_KEY=
# EVIDENCE_SIGNING_KEY_ID=evidence-2026-06     # any label you choose (for rotation)


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# LOCAL-ONLY — used by `npm run` scripts on your machine; NEVER set on Railway
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD=        # db:* scripts → Supabase → Settings → Database → Password
# SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=       # db:apply / db:types without Docker → Supabase → Account → Access Tokens
# SEED_OWNER_EMAIL=you@example.com   # `npm run db:seed-owner` target login

# --- Git hosts (optional) --------------------------------------------------
# "Open pull request" from the workspace. Each host is a per-user OAuth
# connection; without its two vars the Connect button says "not configured".
# GitHub: register a GitHub App with Contents read/write, Pull requests
# read/write, Metadata read, "Request user authorization (OAuth) during
# installation" ON, callback URL <site>/api/git/github/callback.
# GitLab: an application with the `api` scope, redirect <site>/api/git/gitlab/callback.
# Stored tokens are sealed with GIT_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY (required to connect):
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GITLAB_APP_ID=
# GITLAB_APP_SECRET=
# GITLAB_URL=https://gitlab.com          # self-managed instances override this
# GIT_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
